<article class="env-corollary" id="0556">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0556">Corollary <span data-tag="0556">3.5.7.19</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex and let $n \geq 0$ be an integer. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes $X \rightarrow \mathrm{K}(G,n)$. Here $G$ is a set if $n=0$, a group if $n =1$, and an abelian group if $n \geq 2$ (see Construction <a data-tag="00QX" href="/tag/00QX">2.5.6.9</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated and $n$-connective. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will show that $(2) \Rightarrow (1)$ (the reverse implication is clear). Assume that $X$ is $n$-truncated and $n$-connective. By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="051V" href="/tag/051V">3.5.2.9</a> (and Remark <a data-tag="051X" href="/tag/051X">3.5.2.10</a>), we can assume that $X$ has a single $m$-simplex for each $m &lt; n$. Our assumption that $X$ is $n$-truncated guarantees that the tautological map $X \rightarrow \pi _{\leq n}(X)$ is a homotopy equivalence (Variant <a data-tag="0554" href="/tag/0554">3.5.7.17</a>). It will therefore suffice to show that $\pi _{\leq n}(X)$ is an Eilenberg-MacLane space $\mathrm{K}(G,n)$, which follows from the criterion of Proposition <a data-tag="0542" href="/tag/0542">3.5.5.17</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
